{
  "dom_id": "step-q6671",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following **cannot** be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-10x-4?$",
  "worked_solution": "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-4$ and the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of\u00a0}-4}{\\text{factor of\u00a0}4}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-6$ does not take the above form. The value $-6$ can be interpreted as a fraction $-\\frac{6}{1},$ and the numerator $(6)$ is not a factor of $-4.$",
